scope: src/service/docsRegistry
alignment: unreviewed
description: Build-time filesystem walker that discovers every markdown doc across six content roots and produces a deterministically-ordered route registry with commit hashes and rendered HTML.
intent: >
  Walk six content roots (.aide/docs/, .claude/commands/, .claude/agents/,
  .claude/skills/, .aide/reference/tools/, .aide/reference/hooks/) at build time, discover every .md file with zero exclusions,
  derive stable URL paths and slugs, inject per-file commit hashes from git,
  and return a deterministically-ordered DocRegistry that Next.js can hand
  directly to generateStaticParams. Every discovered file becomes a route — the
  registry is the single source of truth that makes drift between the shipped
  package contents and the rendered site structurally impossible.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every .md file under every content root becomes a DocRoute with a stable, lowercase urlPath, an absPath, a git commit hash (or 'uncommitted' for untracked files), and a deterministic order value.
    - Routes are ordered deterministically — section priority follows DOCS_CATEGORIES array order (methodology first, then each section in the order the categories list gives), index.md sorts first within its section, then alphabetically by the lowercased URL path (case-insensitive) — so generateStaticParams output is stable across builds.
    - The orchestrator memoizes its output via a module-level cache so repeated calls within a single build return the same DocRegistry instance without re-walking the filesystem.
    - "getByKey accepts a DocRouteKey of the form `section/slug` and returns the matching route or undefined."
    - In rendered doc HTML, every relative link whose target doc exists resolves to that doc's live route — no .md suffix, correct section, across content roots. A link whose target doc does not exist is reported as a build warning naming the link and the doc containing it, and is left as written.
  undesired:
    - Any hardcoded list of doc filenames or slugs anywhere in the service — if a new .md file is added under a content root and the site is rebuilt, that file must appear as a new route automatically.
    - Silent failures — a file that exists on disk but is missing from the registry, or a commit hash that silently defaults to an empty string instead of 'uncommitted'.
    - Non-deterministic ordering — routes whose sort position changes between builds (e.g. sorted by mtime or inode order) break stable URL generation and make diffing the registry impossible.
    - A route whose urlPath does not match the pattern /docs/{methodology,commands,agents,skills,tools,hooks}/<slug>, or whose URL path is not lowercase.
    - A rendered link to an existing doc that still points at a .md file path, a relative path, or a route that does not exist — for example a link whose case differs from the lowercase route it targets. A link to a missing doc that passes the build with no warning, or is rewritten into a guessed route.
---

## Context

The site's docs area renders directly from this repository's filesystem — the markdown files that ship with `@aidemd-mcp/server` are the docs. The registry is the build-time bridge between raw filesystem entries and Next.js static routes. It must discover files, not enumerate them, because the guarantee "add a file, get a route" is structural: it must be impossible to add a doc without it appearing on the site.

Six content roots exist today: `.aide/docs/` (methodology docs), `.claude/commands/` (a top-level `aide.md` plus subcommands under `aide/`), `.claude/agents/` (agents, all under `aide/`), `.claude/skills/` (one `SKILL.md` per skill subfolder), `.aide/reference/tools/` (an `index.md` plus one page per MCP tool), `.aide/reference/hooks/` (an `index.md` plus one page per hook event). Each root maps to one DocSection discriminant and one URL segment under `/docs/`.

The commit hash per file is the cite-as versioning mechanism the root intent spec calls non-negotiable. Every docs URL is permanently citable at `https://aidemd.dev/docs/<section>/<lowercased slug>?v=<commit>`, which means the registry must inject commit hashes at build time, not at request time.

## Strategy

**Walk the filesystem recursively with zero exclusion rules.** Use the Node 22 `readdir` recursive API and filter only by `isFile() && name.endsWith('.md')`. No allowlist, no denylist, no pattern matching beyond the extension filter. Adding a file automatically adds a route.

**Derive slugs from relative paths; lowercase only the URL.** The slug is the path relative to `absRoot` without `.md`, with `/` separators, in the file's own case, so labels and titles read `Stop` or `aide/SKILL`. The URL path is the slug lowercased: `.aide/reference/hooks/Stop.md` serves at `/docs/hooks/stop`. Collisions are judged on the lowercased URL path: two files whose paths differ only by case would claim one address, and the registry fails the build loudly rather than silently dropping one.

**Special-case index.md for sort position only, not for URL derivation.** The slug for `index.md` is `index` (not empty string). It sorts first within its section so the section root page is always the first route.

**Inject commit hashes via `git log -1` per file.** Run synchronously at build time; wrap in a module-level Map cache keyed on absPath so each file is shelled out to git at most once per build. Return `'uncommitted'` on empty stdout — this is a named sentinel, not a silent default.

**Sort routes in three tiers: section priority, index-first, lowercased URL path.** Section priority is derived from the position of the category in DOCS_CATEGORIES (lower index = higher priority). Within a section, `index.md` (slug === 'index') sorts before all other slugs. Within the same section at the same index-or-not tier, sort alphabetically by the lowercased URL path, so mixed-case slugs order the way readers see them. Assign the final `order` integer after sorting.

**Memoize at the orchestrator level.** A module-level `let cached` variable holds the DocRegistry after the first call. All subsequent calls return the cached value. This prevents redundant filesystem walks when Next.js calls `generateStaticParams` and page components in the same build process.

**Accept a Highlighter instance as input to renderMarkdown.** The markdown-to-HTML pipeline (remark to rehype to shiki) takes a prebuilt Highlighter so the highlighter singleton from step 5 can be shared across every route rather than constructed once per render.

**Resolve in-doc relative links against the registry at render time.** Each relative link's target is resolved against the linking doc's own location and looked up among the routes by file path — never by string-munging the href — so cross-root links (hooks to tools) and links to `index.md` land on live routes. Anchors are kept; external and absolute links are left alone. A link whose target does not exist prints a build warning naming the link and its doc and is left as written; the build passes, because silence, not the broken link, is the failure mode. Rendering therefore needs the whole route set.

## Good examples

A correctly discovered and ordered route for the methodology index:

```
{ section: 'methodology', slug: 'index', title: 'AIDE', urlPath: '/docs/methodology/index', absPath: '/abs/path/.aide/docs/index.md', order: 0 }
```

A correctly discovered route for a nested command (order values here are illustrative):

```
{ section: 'commands', slug: 'aide/align', title: 'Align', urlPath: '/docs/commands/aide/align', absPath: '/abs/path/.claude/commands/aide/align.md', order: N }
```

A correctly discovered route for a hook reference page:

```
{ section: 'hooks', slug: 'Stop', title: 'Stop', urlPath: '/docs/hooks/stop', absPath: '/abs/path/.aide/reference/hooks/Stop.md', order: M }
```

Correctly rendered in-doc links (the site serves trailing-slash URLs): inside the hooks index, `[Stop](./Stop.md)` renders as `href="/docs/hooks/stop/"`; the cross-root `[aide_observer_event](../tools/aide_observer_event.md)` renders as `href="/docs/tools/aide_observer_event/"`.

A correctly resolved commit hash for a committed file:

```
commit: 'b75834c'
```

A correctly resolved commit hash for an untracked file:

```
commit: 'uncommitted'
```

## Bad examples

A route whose slug is derived from the filename only, ignoring the subfolder: `.claude/commands/aide/align.md` producing slug `align` instead of `aide/align`. This collapses the namespace and produces URL collisions when two different subfolders have files with the same name.

A registry that returns `commit: ''` when git returns empty stdout. This is a silent default — the cite-as URL would read `?v=` with no hash, which looks like a bug rather than a named state.

A route with `urlPath: '/docs/aide/align'` instead of `/docs/commands/aide/align`. This omits the section segment and breaks the URL structure that the nav relies on.

An ordering that changes between builds because the filesystem returned files in inode order rather than alphabetical order. Any non-deterministic ordering breaks the stable-params guarantee.

A link to an existing doc left as `href="./Stop.md"`, or rewritten by string-munging into `/docs/hooks/Stop` or `/docs/hooks/tools/aide_observer_event`. Each looks like a link and passes a markdown render, but 404s on the live site.

A link to a doc that does not exist that passes the build with no warning, or is rewritten into a guessed route such as `/docs/tools/missing_tool/`. The build looks clean while a dead link ships unnoticed.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` -- root intent spec; establishes the "zero exclusions" filesystem walk requirement, the cite-as URL commit hash contract, and the six content roots
- `src/data/docsCategories.ts` -- section priority order used by orderRoutes; single source of truth for absRoot paths and urlSegment values
- `src/types/docs.ts` -- DocRoute, DocRegistry, DocRouteKey, DocSection types the registry must satisfy
